Z||000014||Hairy_Fiero||12-17-2001||12:21 AM||||If you live somewhere with cold weather take caution when adding water to your coolant. You can purchase a ball tester from your local auto parts store for about $4-$8 it will tell you what temperature your coolant is set too. <P>Now if and when you add the coolant, make sure you take the radiator cap off up front then pull the thermostat housing cap in the rear along with the thermostat and fill from the rear thermostat housing. This will help to get air that's managed to access its way into your cooling system while running low on coolant, and while losing a little coolant when pulling the caps. When you see coolant coming out the front cap it and top off the thermostat housing with your new coolant. Put the cap back on the thermostat housing, but leave the thermostat out. Start your car and let the coolant cycle for about 30 seconds and shut the car off. Now crack the radiator cap up front and let out any built up air that may still be in your system out and tighten it once you see water/coolant come out. Top it off again from the rear and repeat this procedure about 4-6 times then you can put your thermostat back in. Make sure your car doesn't get warmed up during this I wouldn't want you to get burned. <P>You definitely want to do it this way. Fiero's can overheat very easily if not burped properly. I know it takes a few minutes to do this, but remember it only takes a few seconds to overheat and crack a head/block/blow a gasket. <P>Good luck! <P>[This message has been edited by Hairy_Fiero (edited 12-17-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Hairy_Fiero (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000015||camon||12-17-2001||01:45 AM||||I'd give ya 1,400 for it (sight unseen). But the costs involved in shipping it would not be worth it to me. I'm sure someone closer to you would snatch it up. Heck if I lived closer I would...if for nothing else the parts alone would make it worth it for me (black exterior, the rebuilt 2.8). Too bad you don't live in Pa!<P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||K2m4||12-17-2001||01:07 PM||||Hey syrin...i had a similar situation with my GT ..it was a noise that wasnt related to RPMs. When it sat and idled it sounded like someone was hitting the block with a hammer. It was very intermittent. Scared the piss out of me ..I thought maybe wristpins bearing knock...i ididnt know! So i jacked the car up started the car and you know what it was the spring that holds the exhaust right after the Cat converter were broken so the exhaust was banging against the cradle. It was an aggravating noise that took 3 dollars to repair...Just an FYI sometimes it not always something difficult. take the time to scope out the problem it maybe something simple!<P><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||3
